! The `ph.x` input that generated `reference.out.ph-o2-fixed-lsda`.
!
!   pw.x -in o2-fixed-lsda.in && ph.x -in o2-fixed-lsda.ph.in
!
! `epsil = .true.` with `trans = .false.`: the dielectric constant alone, on a
! spin-polarized ground state. `ph.x` allows LSDA here -- `phq_readin.f90:546`
! refuses an electric field only for *noncollinear* magnetism, and
! `phq_readin.f90:957` only for a smeared or tetrahedron metal -- so this is a
! real Fortran reference for the nspin = 2 dielectric response, not a
! finite difference.
dielectric constant of triplet O2, LSDA
 &inputph
  tr2_ph = 1.0d-14,
  epsil = .true.,
  trans = .false.,
  fildyn = 'o2.dyn',
  amass(1) = 15.99994,
 /
0.0 0.0 0.0
!
! It gives diag(1.110915996, 1.110915996, 1.198004867), and defumat reproduces
! it to 5.1e-6 as of P70 -- diag(1.11091441, 1.11091441, 1.19799977), in 13
! iterations. The obstacle was not what an earlier version of this comment said
! it was. `dv_of_drho` is one `jvp` of `v_of_rho`, so for nspin = 2 it is the
! second derivative of the LSDA exchange-correlation energy in the two channel
! densities, and 1504 of this cell's 91125 grid points have |m| >= |n|, where
! that second derivative is infinite -- so it came back with exactly 1504 NaN.
! What was concluded was that defumat.xc's spin branch had to be made twice
! differentiable there. It does not: `dmxc_lsda` does not compute a kernel at
! those points either, it *defines* it to be zero and CYCLEs past them. The
! missing piece was that convention, applied to the argument the derivative is
! taken at rather than to its value -- which is why clipping the density to
! 1 - eps had left all 1504 points NaN.
!
! `chi_0` on the same cell is validated separately against a central difference
! of the density to 1.1e-6. Its twin `o2-fixed-lsda-phonon.ph.in` is the same
! run with `trans = .true.`, for the dynamical matrix.
